Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup coverage (≥1 organizer, shaper, terminal)",
    "within-team variation",
    "separability from talent and player counts",
    "talent control behavior",
    "structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal)"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure lineup coverage from play-by-play and role classifications",
    "How to isolate coverage effect from confounding by overall player talent",
    "How to implement a talent control that does not introduce artifacts",
    "Whether coverage varies sufficiently within team to yield adequate power"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-027",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Adaptation quality (pct_ran_BEST - pct_ran_AVOID) vs talent as predictor of series wins: corr(talent, adapt)=+0.124. Per-series logistic: talent_diff_z +0.737 p=0.0213; adapt_diff_z +0.328 p=0.2755. Team-in-series cluster: talent p=0.0095, adapt p=0.2449. Univariate adapt beta=+0.311 p=0.1128. After talent control adapt p=0.275; n~55 series cannot separate coaching adaptation from roster quality."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Persistent Impact Score Level 1 gate: Hypothesis that play-type actions have decaying cross-possession memory (lag-1 ≥ lag-2). FAIL decay-sanity: DRIVE lifts flat (+0.015,+0.020,...); within-team DRIVE lag-1 +0.015 to -0.002. Cohen d ~0.01-0.03; flat non-decaying lifts collapse under within-team baseline; team-quality confound."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-037",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Sequence-memory lag effects with game-state controls: Naive lag-1 PPP effects: d(1)=+0.024 AUC, d(2)=+0.004. SPOT_UP lag-1 survives controls, but DRIVE lag-1 p=0.0000 becomes ctrl_p=0.0911, PNR naive p=0.031 becomes controlled 0.136, within-team lift ~0. Lag-1 effects for DRIVE/PNR disappear with game-state controls / within-team baseline."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-017",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Execution conformance of observed mix vs recommended plan: avg |delta| on leverage buckets 4.5pp to 24.0pp; best matched opener #3 in half the sides. Single-game descriptive study, no outcome comparison."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-027",
      "what_failed": "Separating coaching adaptation from talent in playoff series",
      "why": "Small sample (n~55 series) led to inability to distinguish adaptation from talent; talent and adaptation were correlated (+0.124), and after talent control adapt was not significant (p=0.275)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
      "what_failed": "Testing for decaying cross-possession memory of play-type actions",
      "why": "Flat non-decaying lifts collapsed under within-team baseline; apparent effects were driven by team-quality confounding."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-037",
      "what_failed": "Detecting sequence-memory lag effects with game-state controls",
      "why": "DRIVE and PNR lag-1 effects disappeared when using within-team baselines or game-state controls, indicating team-quality confound rather than genuine carryover."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Within-team baselines are critical to avoid spurious effects from team-quality confounding.",
    "Small samples (e.g., 55 series) can be insufficient to separate structural effects from talent.",
    "Game-state controls (period, score margin, clock) can eliminate naive possession-level carryover effects.",
    "The project has a working role classification (organizer, shaper, terminal) derived from the Structural Influence framework."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "The degree of within-team variation in lineup coverage (≥1 organizer, shaper, terminal).",
    "Whether such coverage is separable from player talent and simple player counts.",
    "How a talent control would behave when applied to a binary coverage measure.",
    "Any direct evidence on the predictive power or practical relevance of lineup coverage."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "With 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons, there is likely sufficient data to detect small effects if coverage varies meaningfully.",
    "The main threat is low variance: many teams may almost always have all three roles on the floor, leaving few 'uncovered' stints.",
    "Role definitions (organizer, shaper, terminal) rely on within-season usage and residual influence, which could introduce look-ahead bias if not handled carefully.",
    "A within-team fixed-effects design, similar to the baselines used in FAM:SF-CHT-029 and FAM:SF-CHT-037, can mitigate team-level confounding but may still be affected by lineup-specific quality."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Actual base rate of lineups lacking at least one organizer, shaper, or terminal.",
    "Power to detect a coverage effect of plausible size (e.g., 0.015 PPP) given unknown within-team variance.",
    "Possible collinearity between coverage and the simple count of high-usage players.",
    "Reliability of role assignments for players near the usage/residual thresholds.",
    "Stability of role classifications when using only early-season data to avoid look-ahead."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no",
    "explanation": "No prior study directly tested lineup coverage; the retrieval returned no directly applicable answer. However, the historical failures strongly informed design choices, particularly the necessity of within-team fixed effects and careful handling of confounding by overall team quality."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "BASE_RATE",
        "text": "If lineups without full coverage (≥1 organizer, shaper, terminal) occur in fewer than ~5% of stints, there will be insufficient variation to estimate the effect with useful power."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Some teams may never field a lineup missing one of the three roles, making within-team estimation impossible for those teams."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-037",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-027"
    ],
    "summary": "No earlier study examined lineup coverage. Related work on adaptation quality (FAM:SF-APPS-027) and play-type memory (FAM:SF-CHT-029, FAM:SF-CHT-037) underscores the danger of team-quality confounding and the need for within-team baselines."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "If the proportion of stints lacking full coverage is very low, the design will be underpowered and may produce unreliable estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some team-seasons may have zero uncovered stints, reducing the effective sample and forcing between-team comparisons that confound coverage with team quality."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Even within team, uncovered stints may occur when stars are resting, conflating coverage with the absence of top talent."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Role assignments (organizer, shaper, terminal) are based on usage and residual influence thresholds; players near boundaries may be misclassified, introducing noise."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Defining roles from the same season’s data may cause regression to the mean in coverage effects if extreme role assignments are less stable."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Using full-season role labels to classify early-season stints creates a look-ahead bias; role assignments must use only prior data."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "A binary coverage measure may be too coarse; a gradient (e.g., number of missing roles, or continuous structural influence sum) might be more informative but is not part of the stated question."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If investigators explore several coverage thresholds or subgroups, multiplicity must be accounted for to avoid inflated false-positive rates."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.3,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The effect of stint-level lineup structural coverage (binary: presence of ≥1 player in each role category — organizer, shaper, terminal) on offensive points per possession, controlling for player talent and game context.",
    "unit": "Stint (possession-aggregated to stint, or possession-level with stint-level coverage indicator).",
    "sample": "All regular-season and playoff stints from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26, excluding garbage time (final 5 minutes with margin ≥20). Restrict to stints with ≥5 possessions to reduce noise.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted via Monte Carlo simulation using pilot within-team variance of coverage and effect-size estimates from previous role studies (targeting Cohen’s d ≥ 0.01). Aim for >80% power to detect a 0.015 PPP difference at α=0.05.",
    "controls": "Team-season fixed effects (within-team comparison). Player fixed effects for the five offensive players on the floor (or their aggregated prior net rating to avoid absorbing coverage). Game-state controls: period (1-4), score margin (linear and quadratic), seconds remaining in clock. Home/away indicator.",
    "falsification": "1) Placebo test: randomly reassign coverage labels across stints within each team-season; the effect should be null. 2) Negative control: test coverage of the opposing defense on own offensive PPP; it should be unrelated. 3) Check that talent proxies alone (e.g., sum of offensive box plus-minus) cannot reproduce the coverage effect.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary hypothesis is the single coverage indicator. Any subgroup analyses (e.g., by half, by play type) will be corrected using Bonferroni-Holm and reported as exploratory.",
    "decision_rule": "Two-tailed test at α=0.05. Pre-register a minimum of 10 stints per team-season with and without coverage; team-seasons failing this threshold are excluded but reported. If fewer than 10 teams remain, aggregate across seasons and test only the cross-team design with team random effects, but downgrade to exploratory.",
    "data_version": "Complete NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons (regular and playoffs), as available on 2026-07-13. Role classifications will be derived from the preceding season’s data to avoid look-ahead; for 2022-23, use 2021-22 data if available, otherwise fall back to split-half within-season assignment with a holdout check."
  }
}

